>>
endobj
633 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 494 0 R
/E (ABSTRACT)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
634 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 494 0 R
/E (For thousands of years, mathematics was viewed as the ultimate problem-solving machine. A question was asked, a clever person searched long enough, and eventually an answer emerged. The universe seemed like a locked box, but mathematics was the key.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
635 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 494 0 R
/E (Then mathematics discovered something far stranger: sometimes the key does not exist. Not because humans are not clever enough. Not because we have not searched long enough. But because the thing we are searching for cannot exist at all.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
636 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 494 0 R
/E (Mathematics, the subject built around finding answers, became the subject that could prove there were no answers. This is one of the most beautiful contradictions in mathematics: the ability to prove failure. A mathematician does not merely say "I tried many methods and nothing worked." That is not a mathematical argument. Instead, mathematics can say: "No method will ever work, because the rules of the universe make the solution impossible.")
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
637 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 494 0 R
/E (This idea appears everywhere:)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
