endobj
646 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 495 0 R
/E (More than two thousand years ago, ancient Greek mathematicians believed geometry was the language of the universe. Shapes were not just drawings. They represented mathematical ideals. A circle was not simply a round object. It was the perfect collection of all points exactly the same distance from a centre. A straight line was not just something drawn with a ruler. It represented infinite precision.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
647 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 495 0 R
/E (The Greeks created a beautiful system: Given certain tools, especially a compass and an unmarked straightedge, one could construct certain geometric objects. A compass allows you to draw perfect circles. A straightedge allows you to draw perfect straight lines. Together, these two tools created an entire universe of possible constructions. But mathematicians eventually discovered something astonishing: some shapes were outside this universe. The tools themselves created limits.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
648 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 495 0 R
/E (THE FIRST GREAT IMPOSSIBLE PROBLEMS THE GREEKS AND THE GEOMETRY OF PERFECTION)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
