/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 499 0 R
/E (The ancient Greeks discovered limits caused by their tools. However, mathematics eventually encountered a much deeper question: Are there limits built into mathematics itself? At the beginning of the twentieth century, many mathematicians believed the answer was no. They hoped to build a complete foundation for all of mathematics.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
727 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 499 0 R
/E (The idea was simple: If mathematics is a logical system, then perhaps we can write down a complete collection of basic rules and assumptions from which every mathematical truth can be derived. Imagine mathematics as a giant building. The foundations would be a small collection of basic principles called axioms. From those axioms, mathematicians would construct everything else: arithmetic, geometry, algebra, calculus, and number theory. Every theorem would be like another brick in the building. The dream was that every brick could be traced back to the foundation. Nothing would be uncertain. Nothing would be missing. Every true mathematical statement would eventually be provable.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
