881 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 512 0 R
/E (Aircraft are indispensable to modern transport, commerce, and reconnaissance, supporting activities ranging from passenger travel to global trade. According to the Federal Aviation Administration, over sixteen million flights are scheduled annually, and this figure is projected to double [1]. Although air travel is far more efficient than it was in 1990, when a passenger-kilometre required 2.9 megajoules \(MJ\) of energy, by 2019 it still required 1.3 MJ per passenger-kilometre, totalling roughly one billion tonnes of carbon dioxide \(CO2\) annually [2]. Between 2000 and 2019, emissions from aviation grew faster than those from rail, road, or shipping, highlighting the sector's increasing environmental impact [3]. As the aviation sector and the energy demands of onboard avionics and sensors continue to grow, engineers face increasing pressure to develop reliable, sustainable power solutions.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
882 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 512 0 R
/E (Wingtip vortices form as air spills from the high-pressure region beneath a wing into the low-pressure region above it. Researchers have explored aerodynamic refinements and energy-harvesting concepts aimed at reducing wake losses and recovering energy from such structures [4]. Although traditionally treated only as a source of aerodynamic inefficiency to be reduced through devices such as winglets, these vortices also concentrate recoverable kinetic energy, presenting an opportunity to convert mechanical stress into small-scale electrical power [5]. One method of harvesting this energy is vortex-induced vibration \(VIV\) harvesting, in which airflow oscillations create structural vibrations that can be converted into electricity using piezoelectric materials [6]. Piezoelectric elements are useful in aerospace contexts because they are lightweight, compact, and able to generate electricity directly from mechanical strain without additional moving parts [6]. Proof-of-concept studies using such materials have demonstrated power outputs in the microwatt-to-milliwatt range, but scaling this technology for practical aircraft applications requires targeted experiments that weigh aerodynamic penalties against energy gains [7].)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
883 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 512 0 R
/E (Despite decades of research aimed at minimising induced drag, the underutilisation of wingtip vortex energy for powering avionics and onboard electronics remains a gap in current understanding. Existing literature includes limited experimentation on the placement of piezoelectric sensors along a wing and on the mediating role of angle of attack. This study tested piezoelectric ceramic sensors at three winglet locations across a range of angles of attack, measuring electrical output and drag change against a baseline winglet without a sensor attached. By assessing whether piezoelectric harvesters can reclaim a fraction of vortex energy without introducing significant aerodynamic penalties, this research aims to expand the design space for sustainable avionics systems and to provide a pathway toward self-sustaining, lightweight power solutions for future aircraft.)
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
884 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 513 0 R
/Pg 35 0 R
/A [1928 0 R 0]
/K [0]
>>
endobj
885 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 513 0 R
/E (Flight depends on the interaction of four primary forces: lift, weight, thrust, and drag [8]. Lift allows an aircraft to overcome gravity and remain airborne. Aircraft wings are engineered with an aerofoil cross-section, in which the top surface is curved and the bottom surface is flatter [9]. As air flows over the wing, the curved surface accelerates the air above while the air beneath moves more slowly, creating a pressure difference that generates an upward force through Bernoulli's principle, in which faster airflow corresponds to lower pressure [9]. The aerofoil's camber, thickness, and angle of attack determine the lift coefficient \(Cl\), which quantifies how effectively a wing converts oncoming airflow into lift [10]. As AoA increases, the wing accelerates flow over the upper surface and deflects more air downward, so Cl rises and lift increases until flow separation causes a stall [10]. Generating lift also produces induced drag, a by-product of the pressure differences that push air around the wingtips [11]. This induced downwash tilts the lift vector rearward, producing induced drag that increases especially at lower speeds or high angles of attack, since generating extra lift requires redirecting more airflow into the wake [11].)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
