892 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 514 0 R
/T (One of the most influential refinements to wing design has been the winglet, a small vertical or canted extension added to a wingtip to alter wingtip aerodynamics and improve efficiency [4]. Winglets reshape airflow at the wingtip, where pressure differences between the upper and lower wing surfaces cause high-pressure air beneath the wing to spill over into the low-pressure region above, forming wingtip vortices [12]. These vortices are spiralling, trailing airflows that generate downwash behind the wing, tilting the effective relative wind downward and forcing part of the wing's lift vector to tilt backward, producing induced drag that the engines must overcome [13]. Stronger vortices increase induced drag and reduce aerodynamic efficiency [13]. Winglets disrupt this circulation by redirecting tip airflow upward or outward, weakening vortex strength and reducing downwash, though they do not eliminate wingtip vortices entirely; they weaken and reorganise tip circulation while leaving residual vortex strength that continues to influence wake behaviour [12]. Winglets are most beneficial during cruise at high altitude and during phases requiring high lift, such as take-off or climb, when induced drag is largest, which explains their widespread adoption across passenger, cargo, and business aircraft [14]. Modern aircraft from both Airbus and Boeing widely incorporate winglet variants, including sharklets and raked tips [15].)
/E (One of the most influential refinements to wing design has been the winglet, a small vertical or canted extension added to a wingtip to alter wingtip aerodynamics and improve efficiency [4]. Winglets reshape airflow at the wingtip, where pressure differences between the upper and lower wing surfaces cause high-pressure air beneath the wing to spill over into the low-pressure region above, forming wingtip vortices [12]. These vortices are spiralling, trailing airflows that generate downwash behind the wing, tilting the effective relative wind downward and forcing part of the wing's lift vector to tilt backward, producing induced drag that the engines must overcome [13]. Stronger vortices increase induced drag and reduce aerodynamic efficiency [13]. Winglets disrupt this circulation by redirecting tip airflow upward or outward, weakening vortex strength and reducing downwash, though they do not eliminate wingtip vortices entirely; they weaken and reorganise tip circulation while leaving residual vortex strength that continues to influence wake behaviour [12]. Winglets are most beneficial during cruise at high altitude and during phases requiring high lift, such as take-off or climb, when induced drag is largest, which explains their widespread adoption across passenger, cargo, and business aircraft [14]. Modern aircraft from both Airbus and Boeing widely incorporate winglet variants, including sharklets and raked tips [15].)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
893 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 514 0 R
/T (The piezoelectric effect is a physical phenomenon in which certain noncentrosymmetric crystals and engineered materials generate electric charge when mechanically stressed, and conversely deform when an electric field is applied [16]. Mechanical strain moves positive and negative ions inside the material's unit cell, the smallest repeating structural block; each shifted unit cell becomes a tiny electric dipole, and when many dipoles align, a macroscopic polarisation forms, producing the measurable voltage that appears across the element under stress [17]. Manufacturers increase and lock in this response through poling, a process that uses a strong electric field to align internal domains so their dipoles do not cancel [18]. This coupling is reciprocal: applied mechanical stress produces a measurable charge and voltage, while an applied electric field produces a small mechanical strain, which underpins both sensing and energy-conversion applications [18]. Relevant piezoelectric materials include piezoceramics such as lead-zirconate-titanate \(PZT\) and piezopolymers such as polyvinylidene fluoride \(PVDF\); these families differ substantially in electromechanical coefficients and mechanical properties [19]. Typical PZT ceramics exhibit d33 values on the order of hundreds of picocoulombs per newton, while PVDF films show d33 values on the order of tens of picocoulombs per newton, explaining why ceramics deliver higher power density while polymers offer greater flexibility [19]. Device-level power extraction depends on concentrating strain at the harvester, through choices such as multilayer stacks, bimorphs, cymbal transducers, or flexible films, so that the piezoelectric element operates near the dominant vibration frequency [18]. Vibration sensors and accelerometers provide an everyday precedent for converting small motions into electrical signals using this fast, local piezoelectric response [20].)
/E (The piezoelectric effect is a physical phenomenon in which certain noncentrosymmetric crystals and engineered materials generate electric charge when mechanically stressed, and conversely deform when an electric field is applied [16]. Mechanical strain moves positive and negative ions inside the material's unit cell, the smallest repeating structural block; each shifted unit cell becomes a tiny electric dipole, and when many dipoles align, a macroscopic polarisation forms, producing the measurable voltage that appears across the element under stress [17]. Manufacturers increase and lock in this response through poling, a process that uses a strong electric field to align internal domains so their dipoles do not cancel [18]. This coupling is reciprocal: applied mechanical stress produces a measurable charge and voltage, while an applied electric field produces a small mechanical strain, which underpins both sensing and energy-conversion applications [18]. Relevant piezoelectric materials include piezoceramics such as lead-zirconate-titanate \(PZT\) and piezopolymers such as polyvinylidene fluoride \(PVDF\); these families differ substantially in electromechanical coefficients and mechanical properties [19]. Typical PZT ceramics exhibit d33 values on the order of hundreds of picocoulombs per newton, while PVDF films show d33 values on the order of tens of picocoulombs per newton, explaining why ceramics deliver higher power density while polymers offer greater flexibility [19]. Device-level power extraction depends on concentrating strain at the harvester, through choices such as multilayer stacks, bimorphs, cymbal transducers, or flexible films, so that the piezoelectric element operates near the dominant vibration frequency [18]. Vibration sensors and accelerometers provide an everyday precedent for converting small motions into electrical signals using this fast, local piezoelectric response [20].)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
