895 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 515 0 R
/T (Vortex-induced vibration harvesting has been explored across laboratory configurations, including cylinders, cantilevers, and oscillating foils, as well as preliminary wing-scale experiments, consistently demonstrating that energy capture from VIV is physically achievable [5], [21]. However, power output is generally modest, typically in the micro- to milliwatt range per harvester, unless the geometry and modal properties of the piezoelectric device are carefully matched to the natural frequency of the structure and the excitation frequency of the vortices [21]. Feasibility analyses emphasise several engineering trade-offs when integrating piezoelectric strips onto winglets: designers must balance electromechanical coupling against added drag and structural mass, while ensuring the harvester does not compromise the aeroelastic stability of the winglet under variable flight conditions [22]. Existing research combining wind-tunnel experiments on realistic winglet geometries with coupled electromechanical optimisation remains limited [6].)
/E (Vortex-induced vibration harvesting has been explored across laboratory configurations, including cylinders, cantilevers, and oscillating foils, as well as preliminary wing-scale experiments, consistently demonstrating that energy capture from VIV is physically achievable [5], [21]. However, power output is generally modest, typically in the micro- to milliwatt range per harvester, unless the geometry and modal properties of the piezoelectric device are carefully matched to the natural frequency of the structure and the excitation frequency of the vortices [21]. Feasibility analyses emphasise several engineering trade-offs when integrating piezoelectric strips onto winglets: designers must balance electromechanical coupling against added drag and structural mass, while ensuring the harvester does not compromise the aeroelastic stability of the winglet under variable flight conditions [22]. Existing research combining wind-tunnel experiments on realistic winglet geometries with coupled electromechanical optimisation remains limited [6].)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
896 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 515 0 R
/T (Piezoelectric harvesters integrated with winglets offer potential benefits alongside limitations. Winglets alone have demonstrated typical cruise fuel burn reductions of three to six per cent, equivalent to tens of gallons per flight hour [23]. Piezoelectric harvesters, by contrast, generally produce nanowatts to milliwatts per device under realistic flight vibration or vortex conditions, sufficient for powering wireless sensors or telemetry, but orders of magnitude below propulsion needs [18]. Key limitations include fatigue of piezoelectric stacks and polymer films under cyclic aerodynamic loading, added mass and structural complexity, low energy-conversion efficiency in aeroelastic environments, and high per-watt costs compared with conventional power sources; these factors can lengthen payback periods or negate fuel savings unless harvesters are lightweight, durable, and aerodynamically neutral [22]. For harvesters to be worthwhile, they must deliver measurable onboard value in terms of sensor power and reduced battery replacement, adhere to strict mass and drag constraints, and demonstrate lifecycle savings that justify installation and certification costs [24]. The most realistic near-term application is therefore hybrid: using piezoelectric harvesters to enable self-powered health monitoring, reduce maintenance logistics, and marginally extend the endurance of uncrewed aerial vehicles, while longer-term materials advances could raise power density and lower cost [25].)
/E (Piezoelectric harvesters integrated with winglets offer potential benefits alongside limitations. Winglets alone have demonstrated typical cruise fuel burn reductions of three to six per cent, equivalent to tens of gallons per flight hour [23]. Piezoelectric harvesters, by contrast, generally produce nanowatts to milliwatts per device under realistic flight vibration or vortex conditions, sufficient for powering wireless sensors or telemetry, but orders of magnitude below propulsion needs [18]. Key limitations include fatigue of piezoelectric stacks and polymer films under cyclic aerodynamic loading, added mass and structural complexity, low energy-conversion efficiency in aeroelastic environments, and high per-watt costs compared with conventional power sources; these factors can lengthen payback periods or negate fuel savings unless harvesters are lightweight, durable, and aerodynamically neutral [22]. For harvesters to be worthwhile, they must deliver measurable onboard value in terms of sensor power and reduced battery replacement, adhere to strict mass and drag constraints, and demonstrate lifecycle savings that justify installation and certification costs [24]. The most realistic near-term application is therefore hybrid: using piezoelectric harvesters to enable self-powered health monitoring, reduce maintenance logistics, and marginally extend the endurance of uncrewed aerial vehicles, while longer-term materials advances could raise power density and lower cost [25].)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
