975 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 528 0 R
/T (Several sources of error should be considered when interpreting these results. Angle of attack was set and verified manually using a classroom protractor before each trial, which could introduce both systematic and random error in the true AoA tested; because piezoelectric output is sensitive to small changes in bending and flow separation, such error could mask real differences between sensor positions or create a false impression of which configuration produced the best output. Sensor mounting also introduced variability, as double-sided tape allowed for slight shifts in position and pressure between runs, which could alter how the winglet flexed at the sensor location and affect both drag and voltage readings independently of the intended placement variable. The wind tunnel itself introduced further limitations: the requirement to satisfy the 10% blockage rule meant the wing model had to be scaled down substantially, which can cause airflow to separate earlier and behave differently than it would over a full-scale wing, and the tunnel accommodated only one sensor at a time rather than an array. Finally, instrumentation error is a relevant consideration, as piezoelectric disks produce high-impedance, often transient signals that require a charge amplifier or high-impedance data acquisition system for accurate capture; a basic multimeter without amplification may underestimate peak voltages or miss brief voltage spikes, meaning the electrical output recorded here may understate the sensor's true generating capacity.)
/E (Several sources of error should be considered when interpreting these results. Angle of attack was set and verified manually using a classroom protractor before each trial, which could introduce both systematic and random error in the true AoA tested; because piezoelectric output is sensitive to small changes in bending and flow separation, such error could mask real differences between sensor positions or create a false impression of which configuration produced the best output. Sensor mounting also introduced variability, as double-sided tape allowed for slight shifts in position and pressure between runs, which could alter how the winglet flexed at the sensor location and affect both drag and voltage readings independently of the intended placement variable. The wind tunnel itself introduced further limitations: the requirement to satisfy the 10% blockage rule meant the wing model had to be scaled down substantially, which can cause airflow to separate earlier and behave differently than it would over a full-scale wing, and the tunnel accommodated only one sensor at a time rather than an array. Finally, instrumentation error is a relevant consideration, as piezoelectric disks produce high-impedance, often transient signals that require a charge amplifier or high-impedance data acquisition system for accurate capture; a basic multimeter without amplification may underestimate peak voltages or miss brief voltage spikes, meaning the electrical output recorded here may understate the sensor's true generating capacity.)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
976 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 528 0 R
/T (These findings are useful for guiding further research into energy harvesting and aerodynamic efficiency in aircraft design. This study demonstrated that piezoelectric devices integrated into winglets can generate measurable electrical output under airflow while producing only a minimal increase in drag at certain placements, showing that even small structural deformations can be exploited for low-power energy generation. The results clarify which angles of attack and sensor locations are most promising, allowing future work to focus on the most favourable configurations rather than broad trial-and-error testing. Future work should test different piezoelectric materials, sensor geometries, and array configurations to determine how power output scales with design choices, expand the aerodynamic test scope to quantify the energy cost associated with any increase in drag, integrate power-conditioning electronics, embed sensors into more realistic wing structures, and conduct longer-duration tests to assess durability under repeated cyclic loading.)
/E (These findings are useful for guiding further research into energy harvesting and aerodynamic efficiency in aircraft design. This study demonstrated that piezoelectric devices integrated into winglets can generate measurable electrical output under airflow while producing only a minimal increase in drag at certain placements, showing that even small structural deformations can be exploited for low-power energy generation. The results clarify which angles of attack and sensor locations are most promising, allowing future work to focus on the most favourable configurations rather than broad trial-and-error testing. Future work should test different piezoelectric materials, sensor geometries, and array configurations to determine how power output scales with design choices, expand the aerodynamic test scope to quantify the energy cost associated with any increase in drag, integrate power-conditioning electronics, embed sensors into more realistic wing structures, and conduct longer-duration tests to assess durability under repeated cyclic loading.)
/Pg 50 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
