/Title (One of the most influential refinements to wing design has been the winglet, a small vertical or canted extension added to a wingtip to alter wingtip aerodynamics and improve efficiency [4]. Winglets reshape airflow at the wingtip, where pressure differences between the upper and lower wing surfaces cause high-pressure air beneath the wing to spill over into the low-pressure region above, forming wingtip vortices [12]. These vortices are spiralling, trailing airflows that generate downwash behind the wing, tilting the effective relative wind downward and forcing part of the wing's lift vector to tilt backward, producing induced drag that the engines must overcome [13]. Stronger vortices increase induced drag and reduce aerodynamic efficiency [13]. Winglets disrupt this circulation by redirecting tip airflow upward or outward, weakening vortex strength and reducing downwash, though they do not eliminate wingtip vortices entirely; they weaken and reorganise tip circulation while leaving residual vortex strength that continues to influence wake behaviour [12]. Winglets are most beneficial during cruise at high altitude and during phases requiring high lift, such as take-off or climb, when induced drag is largest, which explains their widespread adoption across passenger, cargo, and business aircraft [14]. Modern aircraft from both Airbus and Boeing widely incorporate winglet variants, including sharklets and raked tips [15].)
/Dest [36 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3905 0 R
/Next 3909 0 R
>>
endobj
3908 0 obj
<<
/Title (METHODOLOGY)
