/Title (Vortex-induced vibration harvesting has been explored across laboratory configurations, including cylinders, cantilevers, and oscillating foils, as well as preliminary wing-scale experiments, consistently demonstrating that energy capture from VIV is physically achievable [5], [21]. However, power output is generally modest, typically in the micro- to milliwatt range per harvester, unless the geometry and modal properties of the piezoelectric device are carefully matched to the natural frequency of the structure and the excitation frequency of the vortices [21]. Feasibility analyses emphasise several engineering trade-offs when integrating piezoelectric strips onto winglets: designers must balance electromechanical coupling against added drag and structural mass, while ensuring the harvester does not compromise the aeroelastic stability of the winglet under variable flight conditions [22]. Existing research combining wind-tunnel experiments on realistic winglet geometries with coupled electromechanical optimisation remains limited [6].)
/Dest [37 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3909 0 R
/Next 3916 0 R
>>
endobj
