3916 0 obj
<<
/Title (Piezoelectric harvesters integrated with winglets offer potential benefits alongside limitations. Winglets alone have demonstrated typical cruise fuel burn reductions of three to six per cent, equivalent to tens of gallons per flight hour [23]. Piezoelectric harvesters, by contrast, generally produce nanowatts to milliwatts per device under realistic flight vibration or vortex conditions, sufficient for powering wireless sensors or telemetry, but orders of magnitude below propulsion needs [18]. Key limitations include fatigue of piezoelectric stacks and polymer films under cyclic aerodynamic loading, added mass and structural complexity, low energy-conversion efficiency in aeroelastic environments, and high per-watt costs compared with conventional power sources; these factors can lengthen payback periods or negate fuel savings unless harvesters are lightweight, durable, and aerodynamically neutral [22]. For harvesters to be worthwhile, they must deliver measurable onboard value in terms of sensor power and reduced battery replacement, adhere to strict mass and drag constraints, and demonstrate lifecycle savings that justify installation and certification costs [24]. The most realistic near-term application is therefore hybrid: using piezoelectric harvesters to enable self-powered health monitoring, reduce maintenance logistics, and marginally extend the endurance of uncrewed aerial vehicles, while longer-term materials advances could raise power density and lower cost [25].)
/Dest [37 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 3913 0 R
/Next 3918 0 R
>>
